WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.830
SPEAKER: In our
last two episodes,

00:00:01.830 --> 00:00:04.110
we learned how to
manage GK infrastructure

00:00:04.110 --> 00:00:06.440
and the individual
services running on it.

00:00:06.440 --> 00:00:09.000
But as our applications
become more complex,

00:00:09.000 --> 00:00:11.280
more services are involved
in serving user traffic

00:00:11.280 --> 00:00:12.953
and completing transactions.

00:00:12.953 --> 00:00:15.120
It then becomes more and
more critical to understand

00:00:15.120 --> 00:00:17.370
how requests traverse
multiple services

00:00:17.370 --> 00:00:20.530
and how each service
contributes to overall latency.

00:00:20.530 --> 00:00:22.350
This is what distributed
tracing does.

00:00:22.350 --> 00:00:24.420
It captures the latency
of user requests

00:00:24.420 --> 00:00:26.640
and how long it takes
each service in the path

00:00:26.640 --> 00:00:28.300
to return response.

00:00:28.300 --> 00:00:30.690
Today, I'm going to show
you how to capture latency

00:00:30.690 --> 00:00:33.450
in distributed applications
using OpenTelemetry

00:00:33.450 --> 00:00:35.670
and analyze it
using Cloud Trace.

00:00:35.670 --> 00:00:38.430
Welcome to "Engineering for
Reliability" with Google Cloud.

00:00:42.410 --> 00:00:45.183
Let's start by reviewing the
basics of distributed tracing.

00:00:45.183 --> 00:00:47.600
These were originally described
in Google's research paper

00:00:47.600 --> 00:00:49.670
on the Dapper distributed
tracing framework.

00:00:49.670 --> 00:00:52.070
We'll link to it below
if you'd like to read it.

00:00:52.070 --> 00:00:54.013
When a user request
comes in, we want

00:00:54.013 --> 00:00:55.430
to create a trace
that'll describe

00:00:55.430 --> 00:00:57.170
how our system responds.

00:00:57.170 --> 00:01:00.380
Traces are composed of spans
where each span represents

00:01:00.380 --> 00:01:02.750
a specific request and response
pair involved in serving

00:01:02.750 --> 00:01:04.459
the original user request.

00:01:04.459 --> 00:01:07.430
The parent span describes a
latency as observed by the end

00:01:07.430 --> 00:01:08.060
user.

00:01:08.060 --> 00:01:09.830
Each of the child
spans describes

00:01:09.830 --> 00:01:11.930
how a particular service
in a distributed system

00:01:11.930 --> 00:01:14.780
was called and responded with
latency information captured

00:01:14.780 --> 00:01:15.890
for each.

00:01:15.890 --> 00:01:18.513
Now, let's take a look
at a basic example.

00:01:18.513 --> 00:01:20.930
We have a front-end service
that responds to user requests

00:01:20.930 --> 00:01:23.030
and makes calls to
a back-end service.

00:01:23.030 --> 00:01:24.530
It has to wait on
that back-end call

00:01:24.530 --> 00:01:27.535
to complete to return the
response back to the user.

00:01:27.535 --> 00:01:28.910
We want to trace
this interaction

00:01:28.910 --> 00:01:33.640
to understand both total latency
and latency in each service.

00:01:33.640 --> 00:01:35.500
We recommend that
you use OpenTelemetry

00:01:35.500 --> 00:01:36.762
for distributed tracing.

00:01:36.762 --> 00:01:38.470
It's an open-source
project that provides

00:01:38.470 --> 00:01:42.310
a single set of APIs, libraries,
agents, and collector services

00:01:42.310 --> 00:01:44.260
to capture distributor
traces and metrics

00:01:44.260 --> 00:01:45.557
from your application.

00:01:45.557 --> 00:01:47.140
Let's have a look
at how to instrument

00:01:47.140 --> 00:01:48.520
your code for tracing.

00:01:48.520 --> 00:01:51.960
We'll also link to the full code
sample in the episode notes.

00:01:51.960 --> 00:01:54.762
We start by sending up
the Cloud Trace exporter.

00:01:54.762 --> 00:01:56.220
This will let us
send the traces we

00:01:56.220 --> 00:01:59.250
collect to Cloud Trace for
inspection and analysis.

00:01:59.250 --> 00:02:01.290
In the front-end
code, we start tracing

00:02:01.290 --> 00:02:03.210
and create the root span.

00:02:03.210 --> 00:02:04.900
We then make a request
to the back end

00:02:04.900 --> 00:02:06.510
with the appropriate context.

00:02:06.510 --> 00:02:08.850
This is data embedded
in the HTTP headers

00:02:08.850 --> 00:02:10.320
that our back end
can use to append

00:02:10.320 --> 00:02:12.390
subsequent spans to our traits.

00:02:12.390 --> 00:02:14.760
In the back-end code,
we extract the context

00:02:14.760 --> 00:02:18.120
from the incoming request
to create additional spans.

00:02:18.120 --> 00:02:19.980
This allows us to
explicitly measure

00:02:19.980 --> 00:02:21.510
the latency of our
back-end servers

00:02:21.510 --> 00:02:23.750
and create custom spans
if, for example, we

00:02:23.750 --> 00:02:27.840
want to time how long specific
back-end tests might take.

00:02:27.840 --> 00:02:30.180
Now, let's run the code
and have a look at how

00:02:30.180 --> 00:02:32.220
we can use Cloud Trace
to inspect request

00:02:32.220 --> 00:02:36.755
latency for both incoming user
requests and back-end calls.

00:02:36.755 --> 00:02:39.130
From the project dashboard,
let's go to the Products menu

00:02:39.130 --> 00:02:41.990
and select Trace.

00:02:41.990 --> 00:02:44.030
The trace list shows
us all the traces

00:02:44.030 --> 00:02:46.670
that have been captured in our
project over the selected time

00:02:46.670 --> 00:02:49.300
interval.

00:02:49.300 --> 00:02:52.850
Let's switch that
to the last day.

00:02:52.850 --> 00:02:54.560
Then, let's filter
for the traces

00:02:54.560 --> 00:02:57.350
for just incoming requests
for our front-end service

00:02:57.350 --> 00:02:59.960
using the label that
we set in our code.

00:02:59.960 --> 00:03:02.870
Next, let's select a relevant
trace from the resulting list.

00:03:06.340 --> 00:03:07.860
This shows us the
waterfall chart

00:03:07.860 --> 00:03:09.690
with the parent span
at the top and all

00:03:09.690 --> 00:03:11.610
of the child spans below that.

00:03:11.610 --> 00:03:13.110
We can see the
latency for each span

00:03:13.110 --> 00:03:14.485
in the chart to
quickly determine

00:03:14.485 --> 00:03:17.460
the source of the latency
in our application overall.

00:03:17.460 --> 00:03:20.170
To get more information,
we can enable events.

00:03:20.170 --> 00:03:22.170
These are annotations
we've added in our code

00:03:22.170 --> 00:03:23.610
to more easily
understand exactly

00:03:23.610 --> 00:03:25.740
what's happening at each span.

00:03:25.740 --> 00:03:28.860
Finally, we can click on any of
the spans to get details for it

00:03:28.860 --> 00:03:30.945
over here on the right.

00:03:30.945 --> 00:03:32.820
This is how we can use
Cloud Trace to analyze

00:03:32.820 --> 00:03:35.040
both the overall
latency of user requests

00:03:35.040 --> 00:03:36.690
and how they
traverse our services

00:03:36.690 --> 00:03:41.220
to find the primary
contributors to latency issues.

00:03:41.220 --> 00:03:42.750
Thanks for joining me today.

00:03:42.750 --> 00:03:44.600
We covered the basics
of distributor tracing

00:03:44.600 --> 00:03:47.750
and saw how to use
OpenTelemetry to collect traces.

00:03:47.750 --> 00:03:49.730
We then saw how to use
Cloud Trace to inspect

00:03:49.730 --> 00:03:51.800
traces and analyze latency.

00:03:51.800 --> 00:03:53.870
I hope this helps you
keep your services fast

00:03:53.870 --> 00:03:55.160
and your users happy.

00:03:55.160 --> 00:03:56.640
Thanks so much for watching.

00:03:56.640 --> 00:03:58.807
Don't forget to like and
subscribe to never miss out

00:03:58.807 --> 00:04:01.250
on more "Engineering for
Reliability" with Google Cloud.

00:04:01.250 --> 00:04:02.150
See you soon.

00:04:02.150 --> 00:04:05.200
